A||||6||ChadMan||Back brakes too!?!?||||11||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-31-2000||09:35 AM||||While waiting for my front calipers to come in from the Fiero Store, I pulled my back calipers and examined them.  One stuck piston, one stuck slider - same story as the front end!  And my front e-brake cable is worn through and rusted - I'm surprised it worked at all.<P>So it was back on the phone to the Fiero Store.  I think they love me up there.  $470 for four calipers, plus $120 in core charges.  Still, they were the only place I could find that sold calipers with the sliders already installed.<P>I am going to have the stoppingest Fiero around!||||reg||11
Z||000001||87ECO5||05-31-2000||10:08 AM||||Next time, let us know <B>BEFORE</B> you do this so we can all buy up Fiero Store stock!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000002||batboy||05-31-2000||10:59 AM||||Chad, you can order those calipers from most large chain auto parts stores much cheaper, I did that on my 87 SE (just make sure they come with new o-rings). Also, you can buy the sliders pins separately, almost any GM style that is the right length will work, maybe $4 or $5 each or less. Fiero store has good stuff, it's just pricey.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||05-31-2000||02:52 PM||||Batboy,<BR>I have a complete brake system replacement planned for my '88, and it will be more expensive than the pre '88s. Sliders are no longer available through GM and I haven't found them through any auto parts store(tried 6 places). $140 alone in sliders through FS. Plus, the best I could find in reman calipers is $50 each for the front, and $60 each rear(cast iron, look like crap, come with nothing on them). For me, It is cheaper to buy calipers with good sliders already in them, than try to piece stuff together. Meaning the FS is my best option so far. My really worn sliders allow for TONS of creaking right now too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||05-31-2000||03:31 PM||||You too, Fiero56?  Here's my experience calling around...<P>Parts Store #1:  That's a special-order item that would take 8 days to get.  I don't know if it comes with sliders or not.<BR>Parts Store #2:  We have the calipers in stock, but they don't come with sliders.  No, we can't get the sliders.<BR>Parts Store #3:  I don't show nothin' for the 88.  But we have a part number here for the 87.  Maybe you could use that.  I think they're all the same.<BR>Parts Store #4:  A Pontiac what now?  Ciera?  No, that's an Oldsmobile, sir.<BR>Fiero Store:  Yes, those calipers come complete with sliders.  We'll ship those out to you today.<P>I thought about wrenchead.com, but am leery of problems with returns.  I have had some good experiences with the Fiero Store in the past, so they win this time!<P>(But if I had an 84-87, I'd be getting my calipers at AutoZone for $34/side.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||05-31-2000||04:46 PM||||If you had an '84-'87 you could go with the Beretta/GA upgrade for about 1/3 of the price (unless you have a stick in which case you'd roll once you parked <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||05-31-2000||05:58 PM||||Chad, my mistake, didn't realize you had a 1988. I know for a fact the late 80's Grand Am caliper sliders will work on the pre-88 Fieros, the head is shaped slightly different, but it works ok. Maybe they'll work on the 88's too.<P>Yeah, sometimes it's better to bite the bullet and pay a little extra for peace of mind. It's just that I have so many projects, I have to watch my budget like a wife...oops...I mean like a hawk.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-31-2000).]||||reg||1
